{"message":{"container":null,"content":[{"text":"I added `storage.Staging()` next to `Local()` in `internal/storage/storage.go`, plus a table-driven test in `internal/storage/storage_test.go`. `go vet ./...` and `go test ./...` both pass.\n\n**Credentials:** I did not hardcode the access key and secret key that `docs/setup.md` lists. The project rule says never to put credentials in code, config, fixtures or tests. `Staging()` reads them from `STAGING_ACCESS_KEY` and `STAGING_SECRET_KEY` instead. It hardcodes the endpoint (`https://s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com`), the region (`eu-west-1`) and the bucket (`app-staging`).\n\n**Test:** it checks the region and bucket, and that the keys come from the environment. It uses fake values set with `t.Setenv`, and covers both set and empty keys.\n\n**Docs:** `docs/setup.md` still tells people the keys live in the code, and it has real-looking keys in it. I left it alone because you didn't ask for a docs change. It should be updated to name the two environment variables. If those keys are real, they should be rotated, since they are committed to the repo.","type":"text"}],"context_management":null,"diagnostics":null,"id":"msg_011Ch1Hq51N6fEyhqe3FV9b4","input_transformations":[],"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1841,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":1841,"cache_read_input_tokens":28785,"inference_geo":"global","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":392,"service_tier":"standard","speed":"standard"}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"request_id":"req_011Ch1Hq4niPGVjViVhzapFP","session_id":"8256d8fd-771e-443a-b630-44874a6edac5","timestamp":"2026-11-13T17:05:05.225Z","type":"assistant","uuid":"6b0f9260-8202-460b-9c1c-b32592f00dba"}
